# FIXED DEFECT, 2026-08-16. Symptom: lldb, stepping off the last statement of a
# function, showed the author a `.hero` line that does not exist — `two.hero:7` and
# `two.hero:8` in a program with six lines — and in the middle of a file it showed
# the blank line before the next declaration. Cause: `#line N` anchors the NEXT line
# and C auto-increments from there, so a Heroes statement lowering to K lines of C
# claimed one source line and let the other K−1 walk forward one line each.
# `emit/writer.rs` tracked what clang BELIEVES and honoured it only at an `at_file`
# call, so the drift its own module doc described was diagnosed and not prevented.
# Fix: `intent` beside `claim` — what was asked for, as against what clang believes —
# with `line` restoring the first whenever the second has aged out.
#
# Scale, measured before the repair: 550 C lines across 81 of 81 `run/` programs.
# design.md §2 promises "lldb breaks on and steps through `.hero` source lines"; the
# breaking half had a test (`lldb_breaks_on_a_hero_line`) and the stepping half had
# none, and that gap is exactly the width of the defect. Named after the defect, per
# Go's `test/fixedbugs` (CLAUDE.md §9).
#
# What makes this case a test rather than an anecdote is the invariant that scans
# every `run/` golden: `no_generated_line_points_past_or_between_the_authors_lines`
# in `archive/bootstrap-rs/heroes-cli/tests/golden.rs`. Reverted against this tree it reports 550.
# This file is shaped to be the worst case for it — the last statement of each
# function is one that lowers to several C lines, which is what makes the drift run
# off the end rather than onto a line that happens to exist.

function twice(n: i64) -> i64
    doubled = n * 2
    return doubled

function shout(s: str) -> str
    loud = s + "!"
    return loud

function main()
    xs: [i64] @ []
    xs @ xs.push(twice(3))
    xs @ xs.push(twice(4))
    print(len(xs), " ", xs[0], " ", xs[1])
    print(shout("we can be heroes"))
